## Factoring: when NWC improves only because the company borrowed elsewhere

Factoring is selling part of the trade receivables to a specialist (the "factor"), for a fee, to collect immediately instead of waiting for the receivable's natural due date. As soon as they're sold, those receivables disappear from the balance sheet assets: reported NWC falls, reported DSO shortens, the reported cash conversion cycle improves — all indicators that, looked at without asking questions, describe a more efficient company.

**The point is that it hasn't become one at all.** Customers pay exactly when they paid before; the company's TRUE operating cycle hasn't changed one bit. All that's changed is WHO advances the cash: before, the company waited for the customer, now it gets an advance from a factor, paying a fee for the service. Economically it's FINANCING — a loan secured by receivables — disguised as an operating improvement. That's why factoring is often described as **debt disguised as working capital**: an analyst comparing two companies on their reported NWC/CCC, without knowing that one uses factoring and the other doesn't, would conclude that the first has a structurally better operating cycle — when in reality it has only moved a piece of debt out of sight.

